    "result_text": "All 254 tests pass. The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the city-capacity rule for `POST /api/owners` following the existing OfficeFloor function-injection pattern used for the other \"reject\" rules (e.g. `RejectDuplicateOwnerHousehold`).\n\n**New files:**\n- `RejectOwnerCityAtCapacity.java` \u2014 a pipeline step that counts existing owners whose (normalized: trimmed, whitespace-collapsed, lower-cased) city matches the new owner's city, and throws when the count is \u2265 50. Cities are compared normalized for robustness; the owner itself is excluded by id.\n- `OwnerCityAtCapacityException.java` \u2014 a checked exception (so it surfaces in the `throws` clause for escalation matching).\n- `OwnerCityAtCapacityExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 responds **409 Conflict** with a `ProblemDetail`, mirroring the existing duplicate/household handlers.\n- `officefloor/escalation/...OwnerCityAtCapacityException.yml` \u2014 wires the exception to its handler.\n\n**Modified:**\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted a `rejectCityCapacity` step (under `transaction` governance) after the household check and before `assignHousehold`.\n\nThe check runs on the built `Owner` before it's saved, so a city already holding 50 owners rejects the 51st with 409. The full suite (254 tests, including the new `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectsWhenCityAtCapacity`) is green.",
